'''barkeeper''' (bär′kē″pėr), ''n.'' One who has charge of the bar of an inn or other place of public entertainment; a bartender.

'''barken<sup>1</sup>''' (bär′kẹn or -kn), ''v.'' [Sc.; < ''bark''<sup>2</sup> + ''-en''<sup>1</sup>, as in ''harden'', ''stiffen'', etc.] '''I.''' ''intrans.'' To become hard; form a crust.
{{smaller block|The best way’s to let the blood ''barken'' on the cut—that saves plaisters.
{{right|''Scott'', Guy Mannering, I. 171.}}}}
'''II.''' ''trans.'' To tan (or dye) with bark.
{{smaller block|Effie used to help me tumble the bundles o’ ''barkened'' leather up and down.
{{right|''Scott'', Heart of Midlothian, v.}}}}

'''barken<sup>2</sup>''' (bär′kẹn or -kn), ''a.'' [< ''bark''<sup>2</sup> + ''-en''<sup>2</sup>.] Consisting or made of bark: as, “''barken'' knots,”
